detail:
Three ways to bind: **A** events by hand (today), **B** one Property per field, **C** signals. `Property.of` already exists.

**Your props pattern (2026-10-30).** `item.props` holds one Prop per property; each is evented, so a view can take a Prop and keep itself up to date.
```js
class Prop extends Events {
	set(value){
		if (value === this.value) return;
		const old = this.value; this.value = value;
		this.emit("change", value, old);
		this.item.emit("delta", { [this.name]: value });  // saving still bubbles up as today
	}
}
task.props.title.set("Buy milk")
view.append(task.props.title)      // the view stays in sync by itself
```

**Trade-offs.** Props: easiest binding, but one object per property. Fine for pages, tasks and UI; heavy for thousands to millions of records. Signals: reads are tracked while rendering, so no object per property and automatic binding, but a second reactive model and a dependency. Cheapest: plain objects with no events, for bulk data that never binds to a view.

**The mix (the recommended option).** Raw `data` stays the stored truth. A Prop is made on demand (`task.prop("title")`) and cached, so records that are never bound pay nothing.

**Your follow-up.** Never mix Prop instances and raw values in one namespace: events would work differently and people and AIs would confuse them. If Props are used, they live in `item.props`, maybe with their own key marker in lines. Your earlier API: `item.prop(name)` returns the evented Prop; `item.prop(name, value)` sets the value directly. Status: a potential option only, not needed until binding needs it.

**Plain `Property` is the never-saved kind (voice session, 2026-10-30).** `core/Property` (built 2026-10-12) already has both kinds of value you described:
- a value in `data` (read and written with `get` / `set`) is saved and bindable;
- a `new Property(v)` kept as a plain field on the item (not in `data`) is bindable and never saved;
- `Property.of(item, key)` is a live handle on a saved key; writing it goes through `item.set`.

So `view.bind` can take an item and a key, or a Property. Never put a Property inside `data`: saving silently stops (the Property readme's warning). D18 says which values belong in which kind.

**Your clone-swap idea (voice, 2026-10-30).** To make many DOM changes without jank: put a copy (clone) of the element on screen, change the real one off screen, then swap it back. What can go wrong:
- focus and text selection are lost on each swap;
- clicks on the clone do nothing (it has no listeners);
- iframes, videos and CSS animations restart;
- the scroll position can jump.

Cheaper first: batch DOM writes once per animation frame (the `view.bind` helper already plans this) and build big lists in a `DocumentFragment`. Keep the swap for a rare big rebuild.

**Naming.** You're fine with `view.bind`.

